  "api_error_status": null,
  "result": "The intermittent 500s on `POST /checkout` came from prices written with thousands separators, such as `\"2,450.50\"`. `npm test` passes with 7 of 7 tests.\n\n**Root cause:** `parseAmount` in `src/money.js` used a regex that only matched plain digits. A price like `\"2,450.50\"` didn't match, `match` returned `null`, and `m[1]` threw `TypeError: Cannot read properties of null (reading '1')`. The log has 50 matches for `ERROR` or `500`. I read the first 8 error lines and the 12 payloads I listed. Every payload I checked was a comma-formatted price such as `2,450.50`, `1,299.00` or `1,005.99`. The failures looked intermittent only because they depended on which product was in the cart.\n\n**Fix in `src/money.js`:**\n- `parseAmount` now accepts correctly grouped numbers (`1,234,567.89`) and strips the commas before converting to cents.\n- Any other bad input now throws `Error('Invalid amount: …')`, which is what the function's own comment already promised. Before, it crashed with a `TypeError`.\n\n**Tests added in `test/money.test.js`:**\n- Parsing of grouped amounts.\n- Rejection of malformed ones (`abc`, empty string, `12,34`, `1,2345.00`, `-5`).\n- A checkout case with `\"2,450.50\"`, which would have failed before the fix.\n\n**Not changed:** I didn't touch `src/server.js`. Even with the new `Invalid amount` error, a genuinely bad price will probably still come back as a 500 unless the server maps it to a 400. I didn't check how `server.js` handles errors.",
